solution:
Cross multiplying, we obtain $ x+1 = 2(3x-1)$. (This is the same thing as multiplying both sides by both $3x-1$ and by $x+1$.)  Then, we solve for $x$: \begin{align*}
x+1 &= 2(3x-1)\\
\Rightarrow \qquad x+1 &= 6x-2\\
\Rightarrow \qquad-5x &= -3\\
\Rightarrow \qquad x &= \boxed{\frac{3}{5}}.
\end{align*}

We have the system of equations:

\begin{align*}
x + y &= 153 \\
\frac{x}{y} &= 0.7 \\
\end{align*}

From the second equation, multiplying both sides by $y$ gives $x=.7y$. Next, substituting the second equation into the first to eliminate $x$ gives $.7y+y=153$, or $y=90$. Plugging in this value into the first equation in the original system of equations gives $x+90=153$ or $x=63$. Thus, $y-x=90-63=\boxed{27}$.

First, we use the distributive property to expand the first two factors:

\begin{align*}
6(x+2)(x+3) &= (6\cdot x + 6 \cdot 2) (x+3)\\
&=(6x+12)(x+3)
\end{align*}We use the distributive property again by adding the product of $6x+12$ and $x$ to the product of $6x+12$ and 3:

\begin{align*}
(6x+12)(x+3) &= (6x+12) \cdot x +(6x+12) \cdot 3\\
&= x(6x+12) + 3(6x+12)
\end{align*}We use the distributive property again and combine like terms:

\begin{align*}
x(6x+12) + 3(6x+12) &= 6x^2 + 12x + 18x+ 36\\
&= \boxed{6x^2 + 30x + 36}
\end{align*}

